'use strict';
window.COURSE = {
 title:'Foundation to US Healthcare', version:1, pass:80,
 sources:[
 ['HIPAA: minimum necessary','https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/guidance/minimum-necessary-requirement/index.html'],
 ['CMS: professional claim forms','https://www.cms.gov/medicare/coding-billing/electronic-billing/professional-paper-claim-form'],
 ['CMS: institutional claim forms','https://www.cms.gov/Outreach-and-Education/MLN/WBT/MLN7388180-MLN-WBT-1450/1450/lesson02/06/index.html'],
 ['HealthCare.gov: insurance glossary','https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/'],
 ['Medicare: parts of Medicare','https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/medicare-basics/parts-of-medicare'],
 ['Medicaid: managed care','https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/managed-care'],
 ['TRICARE: health plan options','https://www.tricare.mil/Plans/HealthPlans'],
 ['VA: community care referrals','https://www.va.gov/resources/how-to-get-community-care-referrals-and-schedule-appointments/'],
 ['IRS: HSAs and health FSAs','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969']
 ],
 modules:[
 {id:'privacy',title:'Protecting Patient Information',short:'HIPAA Refresher',time:6,kicker:'A Responsibility At Every Step',objective:'Recognize appropriate access and protect information while working with claims.',intro:'Every claim begins with a person. Protecting their information is part of your work, even when you never speak with the patient.',sections:[
 ['HIPAA in your daily work','The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act protects certain individually identifiable health information held by covered entities and their business associates. In billing, protected health information (PHI) can include names, member IDs, diagnoses, dates of service and payment details linked to a person.'],
 ['Use only what the task requires','For payment-related work, follow your organization’s minimum-necessary policies: access, request and share only the information reasonably needed. The rule has exceptions, including disclosures to providers for treatment; it is not a blanket restriction on clinical care.'],
 ['A practical pause before opening an account','Confirm your work purpose and authority. Use approved systems, verify the recipient, lock an unattended workstation and keep passwords private. Report a suspected disclosure through your organization’s process. Curiosity is never a work purpose.']
 ],activity:{type:'sort',title:'Appropriate or not?',prompt:'Place each action in a bucket. Drag a card, or select it and choose a bucket.',buckets:['Appropriate','Not appropriate'],items:[['Open an assigned account to research its claim',0],['Look up a celebrity’s record out of curiosity',1],['Verify necessary identifiers through an approved payer channel',0],['Send a claim screenshot through personal messaging',1]],coaching:'Authorized access has a specific work purpose. Verify the recipient and use approved channels; having system access does not mean every record is yours to open.'},cfu:[
 ['You recognize a neighbor’s name in the work queue, but their account is not assigned to you. What should you do?',['Open it briefly','Access it only if your assigned work requires it','Ask a friend to open it'],1,'Recognition does not establish a legitimate work purpose.'],
 ['Which example may be PHI in a billing system?',['A public hospital address','An empty claim template','A diagnosis linked to a patient’s member ID'],2,'Health information linked to an identifiable person may be PHI.']
 ],reflection:'What habit will help you protect patient information during billing work?'},
 {id:'relationship',title:'Patients, Providers & Payers',short:'The Core Relationship',time:6,kicker:'Care → Claim → Payment',objective:'Explain the roles of the patient, provider and payer, and how a claim connects them.',intro:'A patient needs care. A provider delivers it. A payer evaluates coverage and payment. The claim brings their information together.',sections:[
 ['Follow one healthcare journey','Maria visits a cardiologist for an outpatient consultation. Maria receives the service, the cardiologist delivers it, and her health plan evaluates the claim under her coverage. This fictional example will follow us through the course.'],
 ['What a claim communicates','A claim identifies who received care, who provided it, what was done, why it was done, when it happened and which payer is asked to reimburse it. The medical record supports the service; the insurance card identifies coverage information. Neither replaces the claim.'],
 ['Two financial relationships','A payer may pay the provider for eligible services. The patient may owe a deductible, copay or coinsurance, depending on the plan and adjudication. Coverage does not mean every billed dollar will be paid.']
 ],activity:{type:'flip',title:'Meet the three participants',prompt:'Open every card to explore each independent role.',items:[['Patient','Maria receives the consultation. She may also owe part of the allowed cost.'],['Provider','The cardiologist or provider organization delivers care and submits billing information.'],['Payer','The health plan applies coverage and payment rules to the claim.']],coaching:'The patient receives care, the provider delivers care, and the payer evaluates coverage and reimbursement. The claim is the financial communication connecting them.'},cfu:[
 ['What connects service details to a request for reimbursement?',['The claim','The appointment reminder','The insurance card alone'],0,'The claim combines patient, provider, service and payer information.'],
 ['In Maria’s visit, who evaluates the claim against insurance coverage?',['Maria’s employer in every case','The cardiologist’s receptionist','The payer'],2,'The payer evaluates the claim; a provider does not unilaterally decide the plan’s payment.']
 ],reflection:'In your own words, how does a claim connect the three participants?'},
 {id:'patient',title:'Understanding The Patient',short:'Patient Fundamentals',time:10,kicker:'One Person, Several Roles',objective:'Distinguish patient and insurance roles, encounter terms and patient cost sharing.',intro:'Patient, subscriber and guarantor describe different roles. Do not assume they identify the same person.',sections:[
 ['Who is who?','Maria receives care under her husband Daniel’s employer plan. Maria is the patient and a covered dependent; Daniel is the subscriber. Both can be plan members. The guarantor is the person responsible for the account under its arrangement—we cannot infer that person from the insurance card.'],
 ['Before the encounter','Scheduling reserves the appointment. Registration confirms demographics and insurance information, such as member ID, group number and subscriber. An MRN identifies a medical record; an account number tracks an account or encounter, depending on the organization. Eligibility checks active coverage for the date of service. Benefits describe covered services and cost-sharing conditions.'],
 ['Care and dates','An encounter is an interaction in which care is delivered. Date of Service (DOS) is when the service occurs. Inpatient status requires a formal inpatient admission; outpatient care has no inpatient admission. An overnight stay alone does not prove inpatient status. Admission and discharge dates describe the start and end of a hospital stay; statement dates describe a billing period.'],
 ['What the patient may owe','A premium maintains insurance coverage. A deductible is an amount paid toward applicable covered services before certain benefits pay. A copay is a fixed amount; coinsurance is a percentage of the allowed cost. The out-of-pocket maximum limits applicable covered, in-network cost sharing for the plan year; premiums and noncovered services generally do not count. Always use the actual plan rules.'],
 ['A simple coinsurance example','Assume the deductible has been met, there is no copay, and a covered service has a $1,000 allowed amount with 20% coinsurance. The patient share is $200 and the plan share is $800. This example does not use the provider’s billed charge to calculate coinsurance.']
 ],activity:{type:'match',title:'Give Each Role Its Meaning',prompt:'Select a term, then its unique definition. Each definition is used once.',pairs:[['Patient','Receives the healthcare service'],['Subscriber','Holds the insurance policy'],['Guarantor','Is financially responsible under the account arrangement'],['Dependent','Receives coverage through another person’s policy']],explanation:'Explain why the patient, subscriber and guarantor should not automatically be treated as the same person.',coaching:'Match the role to evidence in the account. One person can have several roles, but you should never infer the guarantor from the subscriber alone.'},cfu:[
 ['Maria receives care under Daniel’s policy. Who is the subscriber?',['Maria automatically','Daniel','The hospital'],1,'Daniel holds the policy. The patient and subscriber can be different people.'],
 ['With a $1,000 allowed amount, deductible met, no copay and 20% coinsurance, what is the patient share?',['$20','$800','$200'],2,'20% × $1,000 = $200. Billed charges are not the basis in this example.']
 ],reflection:'Which patient or insurance role will you make a point of verifying rather than assuming?'},
 {id:'providers',title:'Understanding Providers',short:'Provider Roles & Services',time:9,kicker:'Who Delivered Which Service?',objective:'Identify provider types, specialties, claim roles and identifiers.',intro:'A provider can be an individual professional or an organization. The role on a claim tells you how that provider relates to the service.',sections:[
 ['People and organizations','Physicians, nurse practitioners and physician assistants provide services within applicable practice rules. Physician groups and clinics organize outpatient care. Hospitals deliver inpatient and outpatient services. Ambulatory surgery centers provide eligible same-day procedures.'],
 ['Specialties put the service in context','Primary care and family medicine provide routine and preventive care; internal medicine focuses on adults and pediatrics on children. Cardiology concerns the heart; orthopedics, bones and joints; neurology, the nervous system; oncology, cancer; gastroenterology, digestion; dermatology, skin. Radiology involves imaging, anesthesiology involves anesthesia and perioperative care, and emergency medicine involves urgent evaluation and treatment.'],
 ['Roles change with the encounter','The primary care provider (PCP) is often the first contact. A referring provider directs the patient to another provider; an ordering provider requests a test or service. A rendering provider performs the service. The billing provider submits the claim. An attending provider is primarily responsible for care during relevant facility services. A specialist can also be the rendering provider.'],
 ['Identifiers and service types','The National Provider Identifier (NPI) is a 10-digit identifier for covered healthcare providers; it is not a tax number or a guarantee of payer enrollment. A Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN) identifies the taxpayer. Services may be evaluation and management, diagnostic tests, therapeutic treatment, surgery or facility services.']
 ],activity:{type:'match',title:'Read the provider’s role',prompt:'Connect each claim role to its unique function.',pairs:[['Rendering provider','Performs the billed healthcare service'],['Billing provider','Submits the claim for reimbursement'],['Referring provider','Directs the patient to another provider'],['Ordering provider','Requests a laboratory test or imaging service'],['Attending provider','Has primary responsibility for the facility patient’s care']],coaching:'Provider role and specialty answer different questions. A cardiologist may render a service while a physician group is the billing provider; verify both roles and their identifiers.'},cfu:[
 ['A PCP sends Maria to a cardiologist. In that referral, the PCP is the…',['Referring provider','Payer','Facility billing entity'],0,'The PCP initiates the referral; the cardiologist is the specialist.'],
 ['Which statement is correct?',['An NPI and a TIN mean the same thing','An NPI guarantees coverage','An NPI identifies a provider; a TIN identifies a taxpayer'],2,'These identifiers serve different purposes.']
 ],reflection:'Why might a claim’s rendering provider and billing provider be different?'},
 {id:'payers',title:'Understanding Payers',short:'Coverage & Payer Fundamentals',time:15,kicker:'Identify The Program, Plan And Network',objective:'Distinguish major US coverage programs, common managed-care plan structures, funding accounts and the checks that determine payer responsibility.',intro:'A payer evaluates claims according to a specific program and plan. Identify the coverage family, plan structure, network rules and order of benefits before drawing conclusions from the name on a card.',sections:[
 ['The Coverage Landscape','US healthcare coverage includes federal programs, joint federal-state programs and private plans. Medicare, Medicaid, Medicare Advantage, employer-sponsored coverage, individual commercial coverage, TRICARE, CHAMPVA and VA healthcare follow different eligibility and billing rules. Begin by identifying the exact program and plan rather than treating every card as standard commercial insurance.'],
 ['Map The Parts Of Medicare','Original Medicare includes Part A and Part B. Part A helps cover inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility, hospice and certain home health care; Part B helps cover physician and other provider services, outpatient care, durable medical equipment and preventive services. Medicare Advantage, also called Part C, is an alternative delivered through a Medicare-approved private plan and commonly uses plan networks. Part D covers prescription drugs. Medigap supplements Original Medicare cost sharing and is different from Medicare Advantage.'],
 ['Recognize Medicaid Delivery Models','Medicaid is jointly funded by federal and state governments, and eligibility, benefits and billing rules vary by state. A state may pay providers through fee-for-service Medicaid or arrange delivery through contracted managed care organizations. For managed Medicaid, verify the member’s assigned MCO, effective dates, network and authorization rules; a state Medicaid ID alone may not identify the entity that should receive the claim.'],
 ['Military And Veteran Programs','TRICARE is the uniformed services healthcare program, with plan options that vary by beneficiary category and location. CHAMPVA may cover certain spouses, dependents, survivors and caregivers who meet VA criteria and are not eligible for TRICARE. VA healthcare is a separate system; eligible Veterans using community care generally need a VA referral and authorization. Verify the program, eligibility source, approved provider, service and dates.'],
 ['Plan Structure Changes Access','Managed-care labels describe common network patterns, but the actual plan document controls. HMOs often organize care through a primary care provider and may require specialist referrals. PPOs generally allow out-of-network care at higher member cost. EPOs generally cover services only in network except emergencies. POS plans combine network incentives with a primary-care referral structure. Never infer coverage from the acronym alone; verify network status, referral and authorization requirements for the service.'],
 ['Eligibility, Benefits, Referrals And Authorization','Eligibility confirms active enrollment for a date of service. Benefits describe covered services, exclusions and cost sharing. A referral directs care and may satisfy a plan access rule. Prior authorization is payer approval required for some services. These checks answer different questions, and none alone guarantees claim payment.'],
 ['Payer Order And Other Payment Sources','Coordination of Benefits determines whether coverage is primary, secondary or tertiary. Workers’ compensation or automobile liability coverage may be responsible for care related to a work injury or accident, so verify the event and applicable billing rules. HSAs and health FSAs are funding arrangements used to pay or reimburse qualified medical expenses; they do not adjudicate a health insurance claim and should not be treated as insurance payers.'],
 ['Allowed Amount And Patient Responsibility','The allowed amount is the maximum amount a payer recognizes for a covered service under its fee schedule or provider contract. It can differ from the billed charge. For a contracted provider, the difference is commonly a contractual adjustment rather than patient responsibility. Adjudication identifies payment, adjustments and the patient portion. Review the actual remittance and plan rules before describing a balance.']
 ],activity:{type:'simulation',revision:2,title:'Practice A Coverage Conversation',prompt:'Maria asks, “My insurance is active. Does that mean this visit is fully paid?” Choose a response, review the feedback, and continue.',steps:[
 {question:'How do you respond first?',options:['Yes. Active coverage means everything is paid.','Active coverage is one check. We also need the service benefits and plan requirements.'],correct:1,wrong:'Eligibility confirms active coverage, not full payment. Avoid promising a dollar amount before the other checks.',right:'You separated eligibility from benefits. Maria now asks whether approval is needed.'},
 {question:'The plan requires prior authorization. What do you explain?',options:['We need the required authorization, but it does not guarantee claim payment.','An authorization replaces all other checks.'],correct:0,wrong:'An authorization does not replace coverage, coding or other payment requirements.',right:'You explained the condition without guaranteeing reimbursement. Maria mentions a second plan.'},
 {question:'How should the two plans be billed?',options:['Send both as primary and see which pays.','Let Maria choose which is primary.','Verify coordination-of-benefits rules and payer order.'],correct:2,wrong:'Payer order follows COB rules, not preference or simultaneous primary billing.',right:'You have a sound next step: verify payer order and document the coverage information.'},
 {question:'Maria’s plan is an EPO and the requested provider appears out of network. What is the best next step?',options:['Promise out-of-network coverage because the plan is active.','Verify the provider network and the plan’s emergency or exception rules before describing coverage.','Treat the EPO exactly like every PPO.'],correct:1,wrong:'An EPO generally limits coverage to its network except emergencies, but the actual plan terms and any applicable exception must be checked.',right:'You used the plan structure as a prompt to verify the actual network and coverage rules.'},
 {question:'Maria asks whether her HSA should be billed as another insurance payer. How do you respond?',options:['Yes. An HSA adjudicates the claim after the health plan.','No. An HSA is a funding account that may be used for qualified medical expenses; it is not another health insurer.'],correct:1,wrong:'An HSA holds funds for eligible expenses. It does not apply insurance benefits or adjudicate the claim.',right:'You separated the insurance payer from the account Maria may use for eligible out-of-pocket costs.'}
 ],explanation:'Explain how the program, plan structure, eligibility, benefits, network, referral and prior authorization answer different coverage questions.',coaching:'Identify the exact program and plan, then separate eligibility, benefits, network, referral, authorization and payer order. Treat HSAs and FSAs as funding arrangements rather than insurance payers. Clear explanations state what has been verified and what still determines payment.'},cfu:[
 ['Which describes managed Medicaid?',['Only employer-funded private coverage','Medicaid benefits delivered through contracted managed care organizations','A Medicare supplement'],1,'Managed Medicaid is a Medicaid delivery arrangement, not Medicare Advantage.'],
 ['What determines which of multiple plans processes first?',['Coordination of Benefits','The larger premium','The patient’s preferred insurer'],0,'COB rules establish primary, secondary and sometimes tertiary payer order.']
 ],reflection:'Write one sentence explaining why authorization does not guarantee payment.'},
 {id:'lifecycle',title:'The Claim Life Cycle',short:'From Service To Payment',time:9,kicker:'Six Stages, One Connected Story',objective:'Order the simplified claim stages and distinguish rejection, denial, reporting and payment.',intro:'Use these six stages as your foundation. Actual workflows can loop back for corrections, and reporting and payment timing can overlap.',sections:[
 ['Service Delivered → Claim Generation','The provider delivers and documents care. Charge capture records all supported billable services, supplies and procedures. Coding then helps translate that documentation into claim data. Each service line carries related details such as a code, date, units and charge. A claim should reflect the health record.'],
 ['Claim Submission → Claim Adjudication','A claim scrubber can check for missing data and common errors before submission. A clearinghouse may validate format, translate the electronic transaction and route it to the payer. A clean claim has the information needed to process without avoidable delay. A rejected claim is not accepted into adjudication; a denied claim was accepted, processed and refused for payment in whole or in part.'],
 ['Claim Reporting → Claim Payment','The payer reports the adjudication outcome. An Explanation of Benefits (EOB) explains billed, allowed, paid and patient-responsibility amounts. An Electronic Remittance Advice (ERA / 835) sends structured payment and adjustment information to the provider. Claim Adjustment Reason Codes (CARCs) explain financial adjustments; Remittance Advice Remark Codes (RARCs) add detail. An ERA reports the result; it does not itself transfer funds.'],
 ['A claim can take another pass','A rejected claim may need correction before adjudication. A denial may need review and a permitted next action. At this foundation stage, recognize the distinction and where the response belongs; detailed follow-up strategy comes in separate training.']
 ],activity:{type:'sequence',title:'Build The Claim Life Cycle',prompt:'Move the six unique stages into the simplified course sequence. Use the up and down controls.',items:['Service Delivered','Claim Generation','Claim Submission','Claim Adjudication','Claim Reporting','Claim Payment'],explanation:'Explain the difference between a rejected claim and a denied claim.',coaching:'The sequence is Service Delivered → Claim Generation → Claim Submission → Claim Adjudication → Claim Reporting → Claim Payment. Submission does not prove acceptance; reporting explains an outcome and is distinct from receiving funds.'},cfu:[
 ['A clearinghouse stops a claim for an invalid member ID before adjudication. This is a…',['Payment','Denial after coverage review','Rejection'],2,'A front-end rejection prevents the claim from entering normal adjudication.'],
 ['What does an ERA communicate?',['Remittance and adjustment information','Only the appointment date','A guarantee that every claim was paid in full'],0,'An ERA explains adjudication results and is distinct from the funds transfer.']
 ],reflection:'What would you check before saying a submitted claim has been adjudicated?'},
 {id:'billing',title:'One Encounter, Two Billing Perspectives',short:'Introducing PB & HB',time:6,kicker:'Professional Care And Facility Resources',objective:'Explain why a single encounter can generate professional and institutional claims.',intro:'Maria has an outpatient procedure in a hospital. The professional service and the facility resources may be represented by separate claims.',sections:[
 ['Professional billing','Physician Billing (PB), also called professional billing, generally represents services supplied by individual healthcare professionals. The professional paper form is CMS-1500 and the electronic transaction is 837P. The professional may work in an office or in a hospital.'],
 ['Hospital billing','Hospital Billing (HB), also called institutional or facility billing in this course, generally represents the facility’s services and resources. The paper form is UB-04 (CMS-1450) and the electronic transaction is 837I. Institutional billing is not limited to inpatient stays.'],
 ['Why two claims can be appropriate','A surgeon’s work and a hospital’s operating room resources represent different components. Separate claims are not automatically duplicates. Verify the billing entity and the service represented. Billing arrangements vary, so the service location alone is not enough to classify a claim.']
 ],activity:{type:'flip',title:'Explore the two perspectives',prompt:'Open both cards to compare the components of one fictional encounter.',items:[['The professional component','The surgeon’s professional work may be billed on a CMS-1500 / 837P by the professional or group.'],['The facility component','Hospital space, supplies, nursing and other facility resources may be billed on a UB-04 / 837I.']],coaching:'Start with who is billing and what service or resource is represented. A professional service performed inside a hospital can still be a professional claim.'},cfu:[
 ['A professional claim for a hospital-based physician is generally associated with…',['837I only because of the location','837P','A patient premium invoice'],1,'Claim type follows the billing perspective, not simply the building.'],
 ['Two claims arise from one surgery. What is the best foundation-level interpretation?',['Both must be duplicates','Only one can be valid','They may represent separate professional and facility components'],2,'Review the billing entity and component before treating the claims as duplicates.']
 ],reflection:'How would you explain two billing perspectives to a new colleague?'},
 {id:'pb',title:'Physician Billing Fundamentals',short:'CMS-1500 & 837P',time:8,kicker:'Read The Professional Service',objective:'Recognize the main information groups on a professional claim.',intro:'Read a professional claim as a structured description of the patient, insurance, provider and services.',sections:[
 ['Patient, insured and provider','Confirm patient identity and the insured’s information. Identify the rendering provider, billing provider and referring or ordering provider where required. Names and identifiers serve different roles on the claim.'],
 ['Why and what','A diagnosis code explains the condition or reason associated with the service. ICD-10-CM is used for diagnoses in current workflows. CPT and HCPCS identify procedures, services or supplies. A modifier provides additional detail about a service; it is not a substitute for the underlying procedure code. A diagnosis pointer links a service line to a listed diagnosis.'],
 ['Where, when and how much','Place of Service (POS) describes the service setting, such as office, inpatient hospital or outpatient hospital. DOS identifies the service date. Units represent a reported quantity. Line charges and total charges are billed amounts, not guaranteed payment amounts.'],
 ['Your supplied training form','The provided CMS-1500 is labeled 08/05. We use its first page for locating fields only. It has four diagnosis positions and some labels that differ from the current 02/12 form. Do not use this legacy blank as a current submission template. Electronic 837P data is structured differently from a paper page.']
 ],activity:{type:'flip',title:'Read a professional claim in layers',prompt:'Open each layer before moving to the field navigation lab.',items:[['Who?','Patient and insured information; rendering, billing and referring providers.'],['Why?','Diagnosis information and pointers linking the service to the diagnosis.'],['What, where and when?','CPT/HCPCS, modifiers, Place of Service and Dates of Service.'],['How much?','Units and charges. Payment depends on adjudication, not the billed amount alone.']],coaching:'Read the service line as a whole. Procedure, modifier, diagnosis pointer, setting, dates, units and charge describe related aspects of the same billed service.'},cfu:[
 ['What does a diagnosis pointer do?',['Links a service line to a listed diagnosis','Identifies the insurer’s bank','Replaces the procedure code'],0,'A pointer relates the line to diagnosis information elsewhere on the claim.'],
 ['Which item describes where the professional service occurred?',['Type of Bill','Place of Service','Group number'],1,'POS describes the professional service setting.']
 ],reflection:'Which service-line fields help you understand what happened during the encounter?'},
 {id:'hb',title:'Hospital Billing Fundamentals',short:'UB-04 & 837I',time:9,kicker:'Read The Facility Encounter',objective:'Recognize institutional claim dates, codes, provider roles and service lines.',intro:'Institutional claims add facility-specific information that helps describe an inpatient stay or outpatient service.',sections:[
 ['The encounter and billing period','Confirm the patient, facility and payer. Statement From/Through dates describe the period covered by the bill; admission date describes the start of admission. Discharge status identifies the patient’s disposition at the end of the statement period. Do not assume a statement date is always the admission or discharge date.'],
 ['Type of Bill and patient status','Type of Bill combines facility type, bill classification and frequency information; the paper representation includes a leading zero. Patient status describes disposition, such as discharge or continued care. These fields help the payer interpret the institutional bill.'],
 ['Revenue And Procedure Information','Revenue codes identify service or accommodation categories, such as room and board, emergency room, laboratory, radiology, pharmacy, operating room and therapy. Many facility charge items originate in the Charge Description Master, or chargemaster, before appearing on a claim service line. HCPCS/CPT may be reported where applicable. Units and charges appear on service lines. Diagnoses use ICD-10-CM in current workflows; inpatient hospital procedures use ICD-10-PCS when applicable.'],
 ['Providers and reimbursement concepts','The attending provider has primary responsibility for relevant facility care; operating and other provider fields identify additional roles. A Diagnosis-Related Group (DRG) groups certain inpatient stays by clinical characteristics and resource use for reimbursement. It is not a universal payment method for every hospital claim, and outpatient claims are not simply assigned an inpatient DRG.'],
 ['Where discharge information lives','The supplied UB-04 shows discharge hour in field 16 and patient discharge status in field 17. It does not have a standalone box labeled “discharge date.” Use the applicable statement-through and discharge-status instructions and the encounter record together.']
 ],activity:{type:'flip',title:'Explore facility-specific terms',prompt:'Open each independent concept.',items:[['Type of Bill','Facility type, bill classification and claim frequency help describe the institutional bill.'],['Revenue code','A category of accommodation or service, not a substitute for every procedure detail.'],['Patient status','The patient’s disposition at the end of the statement period.'],['DRG','An inpatient grouping used in certain reimbursement systems; it does not apply to every hospital claim.']],coaching:'Institutional claims connect encounter-level information with line-level facility services. Read dates, Type of Bill, patient status and service lines together.'},cfu:[
 ['What does a revenue code generally identify?',['The subscriber’s employer','The professional’s tax ID','A category of facility service or accommodation'],2,'Revenue codes categorize institutional services and accommodations.'],
 ['Which statement about DRGs is accurate?',['They classify certain inpatient stays for reimbursement','Every outpatient claim requires an inpatient DRG','They replace patient identification'],0,'DRGs are a specific inpatient reimbursement concept, not a universal claim identifier.']
 ],reflection:'What information would you review together to understand a facility encounter?'},
 {id:'forms',title:'Claim Form Navigation Lab',short:'Find The Fields',time:15,kicker:'Two Forms, Page One Only',objective:'Locate patient, payer, provider and service fields on the supplied claim forms.',intro:'Open each supplied claim form in a separate tab, then use its printed boxes or form locators to answer the questions here.',sections:[
 ['How To Navigate','The CMS-1500 uses numbered boxes; the UB-04 uses Form Locators, often shortened to FL. Open the form in a new tab and keep this course tab available. Read each question, inspect the form, and choose the box or form locator where the information belongs. Your place saves after every answer.'],
 ['Read The Exact Form','The supplied CMS-1500 is the legacy 08/05 version, while the terminology bank describes the current 02/12 form. This activity follows the labels visible on the supplied first page and clearly marks it as a location exercise. For UB-04, distinguish statement dates in FL 6, admission date in FL 12, discharge hour in FL 16 and patient status in FL 17.']
 ],activity:{type:'forms',title:'Navigate The Claim Forms',explanation:'Explain how knowing the difference between a CMS-1500 box and a UB-04 form locator can improve claim review.',coaching:'Follow the labels on the actual form. Patient, insured and provider roles are distinct. A diagnosis is not a procedure, a line charge is not a payment, and statement dates do not automatically equal admission and discharge dates.'},cfu:[
 ['On the supplied CMS-1500, which is the billing provider area?',['24J','33','21'],1,'33 is billing provider information; 24J is rendering provider and 21 is diagnosis.'],
 ['On the supplied UB-04, which pair is correct?',['42 = revenue code; 50 = payer name','42 = patient name; 50 = attending provider','42 = diagnosis; 50 = discharge hour'],0,'Use the printed field numbers to orient yourself.']
 ],reflection:'Which pair of fields was easiest to confuse, and how will you distinguish them?'},
 {id:'compare',title:'PB & HB: Bring The Terms Together',short:'Shared & Distinct Terminology',time:7,kicker:'Compare Meaning, Not Just Labels',objective:'Distinguish shared claim terminology from form-specific concepts.',intro:'Professional and institutional claims share core information but organize some details differently.',sections:[
 ['A shared foundation','Both can identify patients, insured members, payers, providers, diagnoses, service dates, units and charges. Both can involve authorizations and patient responsibility. CPT/HCPCS appears in professional billing and institutional billing where applicable.'],
 ['Professional emphasis','CMS-1500 and 837P represent professional billing. Place of Service is an explicit professional-claim concept, alongside rendering-provider and service-line detail. A professional claim may represent care in a hospital.'],
 ['Institutional emphasis','UB-04 and 837I represent institutional billing. Type of Bill, revenue codes and patient discharge status describe facility context. DRGs may apply to certain inpatient reimbursement. Do not turn these associations into a rule that all claims in one category look identical.']
 ],activity:{type:'sort',title:'PB, HB Or Shared?',prompt:'Sort the terms into three fixed categories. Drag a card, or select it and choose a bucket.',buckets:['More Specific To PB','More Specific To HB','Shared By PB & HB'],items:[['CMS-1500 / 837P',0],['UB-04 / 837I',1],['Place of Service field',0],['Type of Bill',1],['Revenue codes',1],['ICD-10-CM diagnoses',2],['CPT/HCPCS where applicable',2],['Units and charges',2]],explanation:'Explain one item shared by PB and HB and one item that is more specific to either form.',coaching:'Shared information does not imply identical form layouts. CPT/HCPCS can occur in both, while revenue codes and Type of Bill are institutional concepts.'},cfu:[
 ['Which is shared by professional and institutional claims?',['Type of Bill','CMS-1500 form layout','Diagnosis information'],2,'Both describe diagnoses even though the form layouts differ.'],
 ['Which electronic transaction is institutional?',['837I','837P','835'],0,'837I is institutional, 837P is professional, and 835 is remittance information.']
 ],reflection:'Name one similarity and one difference between professional and institutional claims.'},
 {id:'rcm',title:'Revenue Cycle Management Overview',short:'RCM Overview',time:10,kicker:'The Capstone: Connect Every Function',objective:'Identify the main Patient Access, mid-cycle and back-end functions and explain how they work together to support a complete and accurate revenue cycle.',intro:'Revenue Cycle Management (RCM) is the connected financial process that begins before a patient receives care and continues until payer and patient balances are resolved.',rcmStages:[
 {stage:'Front End',title:'Patient Access',summary:'Prepare the patient, coverage and account before or at the point of care.',functions:[['Scheduling','Arranges the service and captures the information needed to begin the encounter.','Maria’s imaging appointment is reserved for March 10.'],['Intake','Collects the patient, contact, coverage and reason-for-visit information.','Maria provides her contact details, insurance card and reason for the visit.'],['Registration','Creates or updates the patient and encounter record using accurate demographic and insurance data.','Staff confirm Maria’s address, member ID and subscriber before creating the encounter.'],['Prior Authorization','Obtains required payer approval before a service when the plan requires it; authorization does not guarantee payment.','The payer approves the requested MRI for the specified provider and dates.'],['Verification Of Benefits And Eligibility','Confirms active coverage for the date of service and reviews the plan benefits that may apply.','The response shows active coverage and a diagnostic-imaging coinsurance benefit for March 10.'],['Cost Estimate','Uses available benefit and service information to estimate the patient’s expected financial responsibility.','Maria receives an estimate based on the expected allowed amount and her remaining deductible.']]},
 {stage:'Mid-Cycle',title:'Turn Care Into Billable Information',summary:'Translate documented care into accurate codes and charges.',functions:[['Medical Coding','Assigns diagnosis and procedure codes supported by the health record and applicable coding rules.','The coder assigns the supported diagnosis and imaging procedure codes from the completed record.'],['Charge Entry','Records billable services, units and charges so they can move into claim creation.','One completed imaging service and its charge are entered for billing.']]},
 {stage:'Back End',title:'Billing And Account Resolution',summary:'Submit claims, record outcomes and work remaining balances to resolution.',functions:[['Billing','Creates, validates and submits claims with the required patient, provider, service and payer information.','A validated claim for Maria’s imaging service is sent to the correct payer.'],['Payment Posting','Records payer and patient payments, adjustments and remittance details against the correct account.','The payer payment, contractual adjustment and coinsurance are posted from the remittance.'],['Collections','Follows outstanding balances and takes the appropriate next action toward resolution.','A specialist reviews an unpaid balance and follows up with the responsible payer.'],['Denial Management','Identifies why a claim was denied, corrects or appeals when appropriate and uses trends to prevent recurrence.','A denied line is researched, corrected with the required information and resubmitted when appropriate.'],['Self-Pay Or Patient Services','Communicates patient balances, answers billing questions and supports payment or approved financial-assistance options.','A representative explains Maria’s statement and available approved payment options.']]}
 ],sections:[
 ['Patient Access Creates The Financial Foundation','Patient Access includes Scheduling, Intake, Registration, Prior Authorization, Verification of Benefits and Eligibility, and Cost Estimate. Accuracy here supports appropriate access to care and cleaner downstream billing. A wrong member ID, missed authorization or incomplete registration can delay or prevent reimbursement.'],
 ['The Mid-Cycle Converts Care Into Charges','Medical Coding translates the documented encounter into diagnosis and procedure codes. Charge Entry records the services, units and charges that will become billable claim information. Documentation must support both functions; a service that is undocumented, miscoded or missing from charge entry can produce an inaccurate claim.'],
 ['The Back End Moves Accounts To Resolution','Billing prepares and submits the claim. Payment Posting records payer and patient transactions. Collections follows unpaid balances. Denial Management researches and resolves adverse payer outcomes. Self-Pay or Patient Services supports patients with statements, questions, balances and available payment options.'],
 ['Every Function Affects The Next','RCM functions form one connected system. Patient Access data flows into coding, charge entry and billing. Payer responses flow into posting, collections, denial work and patient services. A problem discovered at the back end may have begun earlier, so effective teams trace the cause and share what they learn across the cycle.']
 ],activity:{revision:2,type:'sort',title:'Place The Main RCM Functions',prompt:'Sort every function into Patient Access, the Mid-Cycle or the Back End.',buckets:['Patient Access','Mid-Cycle','Back End'],items:[['Scheduling',0],['Intake',0],['Registration',0],['Prior Authorization',0],['Verification Of Benefits And Eligibility',0],['Cost Estimate',0],['Medical Coding',1],['Charge Entry',1],['Billing',2],['Payment Posting',2],['Collections',2],['Denial Management',2],['Self-Pay Or Patient Services',2]],explanation:'Choose one Patient Access function and explain how an error there could affect a mid-cycle or back-end function.',coaching:'Patient Access builds the account and confirms coverage requirements. Medical Coding and Charge Entry translate documented care into billable information. Billing, Payment Posting, Collections, Denial Management, and Self-Pay or Patient Services work claim and patient balances toward resolution. Problems can travel downstream, so trace the root cause across functions.'},cfu:[
 ['Which set contains only Patient Access functions?',['Scheduling, Registration and Verification of Benefits and Eligibility','Medical Coding, Charge Entry and Billing','Payment Posting, Collections and Denial Management'],0,'Patient Access prepares the patient, coverage and account through functions such as Scheduling, Intake, Registration, Prior Authorization, benefit and eligibility verification, and Cost Estimate.'],
 ['Where do Medical Coding and Charge Entry belong in this course’s RCM model?',['Patient Access','Mid-Cycle','Back End'],1,'Medical Coding and Charge Entry convert documented care into coded, billable information before billing submits the claim.']
 ],reflection:'Choose one patient encounter and trace it through at least one Patient Access function, one mid-cycle function and two back-end functions. What information passes from one function to the next?'}
 ]
};
COURSE.sectionExamples={
 privacy:[
  'A billing specialist sees a patient name, member ID, diagnosis and payment amount on an assigned claim. Because the details identify a person and relate to care and payment, the specialist treats them as PHI.',
  'To research a missing payment, the specialist opens the assigned claim and remittance details needed for that task instead of browsing unrelated clinical notes.',
  'Before sending requested claim information through an approved payer portal, the specialist verifies the payer contact, the account and the specific documents required.'
 ],
 relationship:[
  'Maria receives a cardiology consultation, the cardiologist documents and bills the service, and Maria’s health plan evaluates the resulting claim.',
  'A professional claim reports Maria as the patient, the cardiologist as the rendering provider, the consultation procedure, the diagnosis supporting the visit, the date of service and the payer being billed.',
  'The provider bills $300, the plan allows $220, pays $176 and assigns $44 as coinsurance. The payer payment and Maria’s responsibility resolve different portions of the same allowed amount.'
 ],
 patient:[
  'Maria is the patient and dependent, Daniel is the subscriber, and the account identifies Daniel as guarantor. These roles are verified separately because a subscriber is not automatically the guarantor.',
  'At scheduling, Maria provides the reason for her visit. At registration, staff confirm her address, member ID and subscriber. The system assigns an encounter account while her MRN continues to identify her medical record. An eligibility response confirms active coverage and the benefits response describes the specialist copay.',
  'Maria’s office consultation on March 10 is an outpatient encounter with March 10 as the Date of Service. A later formally admitted hospital stay has admission and discharge dates, while the hospital claim may show a statement period covering several dates.',
  'Maria pays a monthly premium to maintain coverage. Her plan may require a $500 deductible, a $30 specialist copay and 20% coinsurance until applicable in-network spending reaches the plan’s out-of-pocket maximum.',
  'For a covered service with a $1,000 allowed amount, a met deductible, no copay and 20% coinsurance, Maria owes $200 and the plan portion is $800.'
 ],
 providers:[
  'A family-medicine physician sees Maria in a clinic, a radiologist interprets her imaging, and a hospital provides the facility, nursing and equipment for a later inpatient stay.',
  'Maria’s PCP manages routine care, a cardiologist evaluates her heart condition, a radiologist reads an echocardiogram and an anesthesiologist supports a surgical procedure.',
  'Maria’s PCP refers her to a cardiologist. The cardiologist orders an imaging test, a radiologist renders the interpretation, the radiology group bills it, and the hospitalist is the attending provider during an admission.',
  'The cardiologist’s NPI identifies the healthcare provider, while the physician group’s TIN identifies the taxpayer. A claim may report an evaluation and management service plus a diagnostic test.'
 ],
 payers:[
  'A 70-year-old may have Original Medicare, a child may have a state Medicaid managed-care plan, an employee may have commercial employer coverage, and an eligible military family member may use TRICARE. The exact program and plan determine the next billing check.',
  'After an inpatient hospital stay, Part A may apply to the facility services while Part B may apply to a physician’s professional service. Another beneficiary may receive Medicare benefits through a Medicare Advantage plan and prescription coverage through that plan or Part D.',
  'A member presents a state Medicaid ID, but the eligibility response shows enrollment in a contracted managed-care organization. The provider verifies that MCO and sends the claim according to its network and authorization rules.',
  'An eligible TRICARE beneficiary follows the rules of the selected TRICARE plan. A CHAMPVA beneficiary is verified through that program. A Veteran receiving authorized community care is matched to the VA referral, approved service and approved dates.',
  'An HMO member may need a PCP referral before seeing a specialist. A PPO member may use an out-of-network specialist at a higher cost. An EPO member generally stays in network except for emergencies, while a POS plan may combine network benefits with referral requirements.',
  'Eligibility confirms that Maria’s plan is active on March 10. Benefits show specialist coverage and cost sharing. A referral directs her to cardiology, while prior authorization approves a required imaging service. None of these checks alone promises payment.',
  'Maria’s employer plan is primary and her spouse’s plan is secondary under Coordination of Benefits. Her HSA may later help pay an eligible patient balance, but it does not adjudicate the insurance claim.',
  'A provider bills $500, the contracted plan allows $320, applies a $180 contractual adjustment, pays $256 and assigns $64 as coinsurance. The remittance, contract and plan rules support each amount.'
 ],
 lifecycle:[
  'A clinician completes Maria’s consultation and documents it. The billing system then combines patient, provider, diagnosis, procedure, date and charge information to generate the claim.',
  'The provider sends the claim through a clearinghouse. The payer receives it, applies coverage and payment rules, and decides the allowed amount, payment, adjustments and patient responsibility.',
  'The payer sends an ERA to the provider and an EOB to Maria explaining the processed claim. The electronic payment arrives separately and is posted to the account.',
  'A claim missing a required member ID may be rejected before adjudication and corrected for resubmission. A claim denied after adjudication may require correction, additional documentation or an appeal, depending on the reason.'
 ],
 billing:[
  'The cardiologist reports the professional evaluation and interpretation on a CMS-1500 or 837P, including the rendering provider and service-line details.',
  'The hospital reports the facility, nursing, equipment and departmental services on a UB-04 or 837I using institutional information such as Type of Bill and revenue codes.',
  'For one emergency visit, the hospital may bill the facility component and the emergency physician group may bill the professional component. Matching dates alone do not make the claims duplicates.'
 ],
 pb:[
  'CMS-1500 box 2 identifies Maria as the patient, box 1a reports the insured ID, box 24J identifies the rendering provider and box 33 identifies the billing provider.',
  'An ICD-10-CM diagnosis explains why Maria was evaluated, while a CPT code reports what professional service the clinician performed. A modifier may add a relevant circumstance without replacing the procedure code.',
  'Box 24A reports the Date of Service, box 24B reports Place of Service, box 24G reports units and box 24F reports the line charge for the professional service.',
  'A learner opens the supplied first page and finds the patient name in box 2, diagnosis information in box 21 and the billing provider area in box 33.'
 ],
 hb:[
  'A hospital claim uses the statement From and Through dates to describe the billing period and the admission date to show when an inpatient stay began. These dates may differ.',
  'A Type of Bill can describe the facility category, bill classification and frequency, while patient status reports the patient’s disposition, such as discharge to home.',
  'A room-and-board revenue code categorizes the facility service. A HCPCS code may identify a specific outpatient service, while service units and line charges report quantity and amount.',
  'The hospital reports its billing-provider NPI and the attending provider. For an eligible inpatient stay, a DRG may group the case for reimbursement based on clinical and other factors.',
  'The UB-04 can show the statement period in FL 6, admission date in FL 12, discharge hour in FL 16 and patient status in FL 17. Patient status is not a separate discharge-date field.'
 ],
 forms:[
  'To answer where the rendering provider identifier belongs, the learner opens the supplied CMS-1500 first page, scans the numbered service-line boxes and selects box 24J in the course.',
  'On the supplied UB-04, the learner distinguishes FL 6 for the statement period from FL 12 for admission date and FL 17 for patient status before answering.'
 ],
 compare:[
  'Both a professional claim and an institutional claim can report Maria’s name, diagnosis, service date, units and charges even though those details appear in different locations.',
  'A cardiologist’s professional claim uses CMS-1500 or 837P and reports Place of Service and rendering-provider details for the consultation.',
  'The hospital’s institutional claim uses UB-04 or 837I and may report Type of Bill, revenue codes and patient status for the related facility encounter.'
 ],
 rcm:[
  'Patient Access schedules Maria’s imaging, completes intake and registration, verifies active coverage and benefits, obtains required authorization and provides a cost estimate before the service.',
  'After the imaging is documented, Medical Coding assigns the supported codes and Charge Entry records the service, units and charge for claim creation.',
  'Billing submits the claim, Payment Posting records the remittance, Denial Management works a denied line, Collections follows the unresolved balance, and Patient Services explains an eligible self-pay amount to Maria.',
  'If Billing discovers an invalid member ID, the team traces it to Registration, corrects the account and shares the finding so Patient Access can prevent similar downstream rejections.'
 ]
};
