COURSE.exam=[
 ['You are assigned a claim inquiry. What should guide access to patient information?',['Everything available in the record','Only information reasonably needed under your authorized work and policy','Whether you know the patient'],1,'Use authorized access and minimum-necessary policies for payment-related work.','HIPAA'],
 ['A colleague asks you to send a claim through a personal chat app. What is the best response?',['Send only the first page','Remove the diagnosis but leave all identifiers','Use an approved channel and verify the recipient'],2,'A personal channel is not made appropriate by sending less of the claim.','HIPAA'],
 ['Which description correctly connects the three participants?',['Patient receives care; provider delivers it; payer evaluates coverage and reimbursement','Patient adjudicates; provider buys coverage; payer treats','Provider and payer always mean the same organization'],0,'The three participants have different roles connected by a claim.','Relationships'],
 ['A child receives care under a parent’s policy. Which fact can be concluded?',['The child is always the guarantor','The parent is the patient','The child is the patient and the parent is the subscriber'],2,'Do not infer the guarantor; the account arrangement determines it.','Patient'],
 ['An eligible covered service has a $500 allowed amount, deductible met, no copay and 20% coinsurance. What is the patient share?',['$400','$100','$500'],1,'20% of the allowed $500 is $100 under the stated assumptions.','Patient'],
 ['A physician performs a service and the physician group submits the claim. The physician is the…',['Rendering provider','Payer','Subscriber'],0,'Rendering describes performance; billing describes submission.','Providers'],
 ['Which statement correctly separates identifiers?',['The NPI is a patient account number','NPI and TIN are interchangeable','An NPI identifies a provider; a TIN identifies a taxpayer'],2,'An NPI is not a tax ID, enrollment guarantee or patient identifier.','Providers'],
 ['Which arrangement delivers Medicare benefits through approved private plans?',['Managed Medicaid','Medicare Advantage','All employer plans'],1,'Medicare Advantage is distinct from Medicaid managed care.','Payers'],
 ['A service is authorized. What can you safely conclude?',['The authorization requirement may be met; other claim and coverage rules still apply','The payer must pay the full charge','No eligibility check is necessary'],0,'Authorization does not guarantee payment.','Payers'],
 ['A patient has two plans. What should determine payer order?',['The newest insurance card','The plan with the highest premium','Coordination-of-benefits rules'],2,'COB determines primary and subsequent processing order.','Payers'],
 ['Which stage comes immediately after Claim Submission in this course’s simplified sequence?',['Service Delivered','Claim Adjudication','Claim Generation'],1,'Submission is followed by adjudication, then reporting and payment in the simplified model.','Life cycle'],
 ['A clearinghouse returns a claim before adjudication because required data is missing. What is this?',['A rejection','A patient payment','A coverage denial after adjudication'],0,'A rejection is different from a payer’s adverse adjudication decision.','Life cycle'],
 ['Which statement about the EOB and ERA is accurate?',['Both are always patient bills','The ERA itself transfers the money','They explain claim processing; the ERA provides remittance information to the provider'],2,'Reporting explains the outcome; payment and a patient bill are separate concepts.','Life cycle'],
 ['A surgeon bills professional services performed at a hospital. Which pair generally applies?',['UB-04 / 837I','CMS-1500 / 837P','EOB / 835'],1,'Professional services can be performed in a facility and still use the professional transaction.','PB'],
 ['On the supplied CMS-1500, where are procedure codes and modifiers?',['24D','33','1a'],0,'24D contains procedure/service information and modifiers on the supplied form.','Forms'],
 ['On the supplied UB-04, field 17 describes…',['A standalone discharge date','The subscriber’s premium','Patient discharge status'],2,'Field 17 is disposition/status, not a standalone discharge-date box.','Forms'],
 ['Which pair is more specific to institutional claims?',['Member ID and diagnosis','Revenue codes and Type of Bill','Patient name and units'],1,'Revenue codes and Type of Bill help describe institutional services and context.','HB'],
 ['A hospital and a physician group bill the same encounter date. What should you recognize?',['They may bill different facility and professional components','They must be duplicate claims','Both must use CMS-1500'],0,'Review the billing entities and represented services before concluding duplication.','PB & HB'],
 ['Where does charge capture sit in this course’s RCM model?',['Front end','Middle cycle','Back end'],1,'Charge capture translates delivered services into billable information.','RCM'],
 ['A registration error leads to a rejected claim. What is the best overall lesson?',['Only back-end teams influence payment','Intake information does not affect reimbursement','Earlier revenue-cycle work affects later claim outcomes'],2,'The stages are connected; upstream quality supports downstream processing.','RCM']
];
